description:
Eliminates the `role` property, and ensures that the `User` class doesn't have bunch of properties that only apply to user turns. 

Part of #500

@gadenbuie is this going to affect shinychat or btw?

Rather than hashing the complete provider object, we should just hash the most critical components. This trades a little safety for convenience; I don't think there are likely to be too many cases where you re-call `batch_chat()` with a subtly different provider object and this makes it more likely that you can retrieve results stored with a different version of ellmer. I've also added an escape hatch so you can use chats saved by ellmer 0.3.0 in ellmer 0.4.0

Extracted out from #799.

@simonpcouch I think the implementation is straightforward so doesn't need a close inspection, but does the reasoning and documentation make sense to you?

Add a `on_error` argument to match `httr2::req_parallel_perform()` and default it to "continue". Then ensure that all output types revealed errored and cancelled requests appropriately.

Fixes #628

@kbenoit this is the final piece

Now it will warn, instead of failing, if some results are not parseable. This change includes making `ContentJson()` lazier at parsing JSON so we can during data extraction, not object creation. Also includes some tests (and a small fix) to ensure that `NULL` can be coerced to `NA`s of the correct type.

Part of #628

@kbenoit I think this resolves the problems related to data extraction, and makes sense to me as a self-contained PR. Note that from my exploration is looks like `convert_from_type()` already did most of the work needed to generate missing responses provided that you feed it `NULL`.

I've ensured that various special cases (e.g. zero or one rows) don't error, but haven't otherwise tried to optimise their display.

Fixes #744

@simonpcouch @gadenbuie @jcheng5 let me know if there's anything else that you think would be useful here. I'd also love thoughts on the name. Presuambly in the future we might also have a `df_data()`/`df_raw()` or similar that would ship an entire data frame to the LLM in the format we believe to be most useful.

Maybe fixes #666

@rpodcast could you please give this ago? In principle it shouldn't change any logic, but now `models_aws_bedrock()` uses _exactly_ the same code path as `chat_aws_bedrock()`. 

But also note that the endpoints are different: `models_` uses `https://bedrock.%s.amazonaws.com` and `chat_` uses `"https://bedrock-runtime.%s.amazonaws.com"`, so that might also be your issue.

Partial matching strikes again!

Snowflake's "OpenAI-compatible" API always seems to return a final delta with no content. The JSON looks something like the following:

    {
      "type": "text",
      "content_list": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ""
        }
      ],
      "text": ""
    }

Unfortunately this `content_list` is partially matched by `content`, making us accidentally emit it when `content` is missing.

Closes #533.

And use the same internals as `parallel_chat()`.

